target/
artifacts/
coverage/
Cargo.lock

# The seed corpus is checked in on purpose. A fuzzer that starts from real, valid addresses reaches
# the interesting branches — checksum verified, version byte matched — in seconds; one that starts
# from nothing spends hours learning the base58 alphabet first.
#
# libFuzzer writes every input it *discovers* back into the same `corpus/<target>/` directory, named
# by SHA-1. Those are generated, so ignore everything and un-ignore the hand-picked seeds, which are
# the files ending in `.seed`. libFuzzer reads every file in the directory regardless of its name,
# so the extension costs nothing.
corpus/*/*
!corpus/*/*.seed
